Make the systemd --user unit-activation path optional (-Dsystemd=false),
falling back to libelogind for the SYSTEMD_STRICT_ENV version check.

Since gnome-session 50, session bring-up works exclusively through systemd
--user unit activation: gnome-session-init-worker (leader-systemd.c) calls
org.freedesktop.systemd1.Manager.StartUnit() to launch the
gnome-session@<name>.target, and every service (gnome-shell, gsd-*, etc.) is
installed as a systemd user unit under data/meson.build. elogind does not
implement org.freedesktop.systemd1, so on non-systemd inits this hard-fails.

This patch only makes the systemd bits optional at the build-system level
(mirroring the approach taken upstream for gnome-shell's own -Dsystemd=false
option, and adapted from swagtoy's gnome-session fork used by the
gnome-session-openrc project: https://gitlab.gnome.org/swagtoy/gnome-session/
-/commit/c17b5b56bcfcc0eba899295da7c79fb3ac98fd45). It does NOT restore a
working non-systemd session leader on its own -- gnome-session-init-worker
is disabled entirely when -Dsystemd=false, and gnome-session-ctl (which
signals sd_notify/systemd1 unit state) is skipped too. A replacement
init-worker plus a runit-based re-implementation of the session/services
units is still required to actually launch a GNOME session without systemd.

